The code in this repository — the pipeline under `pipelines/`, the library in
`derna2_analysis/`, the tests, and the drivers vendored from the DERNA2
repository under `drivers/` — is this project's own work, as are the manifests
under `pins/` and the provenance notes beside them.

What follows is not this project's work. None of it is code: these are input
sequences and one predicted structure, redistributed so that a published figure
can be reproduced from the same bytes it was drawn from. Most are
byte-unmodified, and where one is not — a header replaced, a fragment cut — this
file says so, because a sequence does not stop being someone's data when its
header is rewritten. Each one's `PROVENANCE` note under `inputs/` is the
authoritative record of where it came from and how it was extracted; this file
names the terms.

  Protein sequences from UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot
  ------------------------------------------
  79 of the 85 proteins this project designs are UniProt sequences.

  77 of them are under `inputs/uniprot/`, with copies of some under
  `inputs/e1-natives/` and `inputs/e3/` where an experiment reads them from its
  own directory. Each of those files is UniProt's own FASTA for that accession,
  byte-unmodified.

  Two more are UniProt sequences whose files do not look like it, and are named
  here so the attribution is not lost with the header:

    inputs/P0DTC2.fasta     the sequence is UniProtKB P0DTC2 (SPIKE_SARS2),
                            1,273 residues, identical to UniProt's; only the
                            FASTA header was replaced by a bare `>P0DTC2`.
    inputs/e3/RBD.fasta     residues 319-541 of that same entry in UniProt
                            numbering, as inputs/e3/PROVENANCE-e2-screen.md
                            records -- a fragment of UniProt data, not a
                            separate sequence.

